  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue with the persistent \n   weather pattern through Saturday.\n\n-  Dry thunderstorm potential: Thursday afternoon through Friday\n   morning across southwest and west-central Montana.\n\n-  Elevated winds and fire weather: Saturday and Sunday region-\n   wide.\n\nAir quality remains the biggest impact in the short term as the\nNorthern Rockies is still in a dry and hot southwesterly flow.\nThis pattern will continue through Friday, then on Saturday a\nstationary Pacific trough is expected to move onshore and squash\nthe high pressure ridge that has dominated our weather.\nHistorically, ridge breakdown after a period of hot and dry\nweather can lead to elevated fire weather concerns.\n\nThursday a short wave ejecting off the Pacific low pressure will\ninteract with a favorable atmosphere: strong mid-level lapse \nrates and weak to moderate shear. The atmosphere is very dry and \nmodels are not excited about thunderstorms, that said this \nparticular setup has in the past produced strong dry thunderstorms.\nThe main threats would be dry lightning and strong outflow wind \ngusts. The most favorable atmospheric setup is south and east of \nthe Missoula Valley especially along the Continental Divide. \n\nAs the Pacific trough pushes through the region winds will\nincrease Saturday. The strongest winds will happen post dry cold\nfront overnight Saturday into Sunday. Expect widespread westerly \nwinds sustaining 10-25 mph with gusts of 25 to 40 mph across the \nregion. This wind/ heat/ and low RH combination will significantly\nelevate fire weather concerns and create hazardous, choppy \nconditions on lakes and waterways. This system will remain mostly \ndry with no significant or notable precipitation expected, but \ntemperatures will drop 10-20 degrees behind the front for Sunday \nand Monday bringing temperatures back to near and just below \nnormal for this time of year.",
